Journa­lists belong­ing to Axact's media initia­tive Bol Networ­k announ­ce resign­ations on Twitte­r
Journalists belonging to Axact’s media initiative Bol Network began dissociating themselves from the network late on Saturday, days after a NYT report revealed the company was running the world’s largest fake diploma mill.
Senior journalist Kamran Khan was the first to announce his decision to resign from Bol Network on Twitter.
Read: Fake diplomas, real cash: Pakistani company Axact reaps millions
The journalist tweeted: “Charges against Axact far from proven in court but my conscience not letting me continue. I’ve decided to disassociate from Bol immediately.”
Charges against Axact far from proven in court but my conscience not letting me continue.I've decided to disassociate from Bol immediately — Kamran Khan (@AajKamranKhan) May 23, 2015
Shortly after Kamran’s resignation, Azhar Abbas, Iftikhar Ahmed, Wajahat Khan, Asma Shirazi and Nusrat Javeed also tweeted that they have resigned from the network.
I can not work for an organization whose basic workings conflict with my proffesional comittment.Ive decided to disassociate myself from Bol — Iftikhar Ahmad (@jawabdeyh) May 23, 2015
I have resigned from BOL after speaking to my editors and staff. I did put together a great team of journalists. Wish them best — Azhar Abbas (@AzharAbbas3) May 23, 2015
It's done. I will be back, but not on Bol. Pakistan Zindabad. Journalism Paindabad. — Wajahat S. Khan (@WajSKhan) May 23, 2015
I have Resigned from Bol News, truth must prevail. — Asma Shirazi (@asmashirazi) May 23, 2015
Earlier during the day, Shirazi tweeted that she will resign immediately if allegations against Bol are proven true.
Investigations against #Axact underway, wl resign immediately if they prove guilty “@IAmBHUTOO: @asmashirazi leave it for your self respect” — Asma Shirazi (@asmashirazi) May 23, 2015
Nusrat Javeed tweeted “Although I strongly feel that my colleagues in media rushed to pass final judgement regarding our relations with Bol. Yet I am quitting.”
He respected sentiments of his colleague Mushtaq Minhas who announced that he could not abandon the channel’s staff and will be staying on.
I cannot leave my 2200 colleagues to prove myself a hero. — Mushtaq Minhas (@mushtaqminhas) May 23, 2015
I m grateful to Nusrat Bhai to understand my position and endorsing it firmly. — Mushtaq Minhas (@mushtaqminhas) May 23, 2015
Although I will never be able to imagine my career without him after along association of 8 years. — Mushtaq Minhas (@mushtaqminhas) May 23, 2015
I will remain until my last breath as a devoted younger brother of Nusrat Bhai. — Mushtaq Minhas (@mushtaqminhas) May 23, 2015
Jasmeen Manzoor too announced that she was staying with Bol, for now.
I will let my viewers know about my decision soon ! I have not resigned yet from bol — jasmeen manzoor (@jasmeenmanzoor) May 23, 2015
Hours before his resignation Kamran tweeted that many ‘respected senior journalists,’ including himself, were reviewing their decision to stay at Bol or not.
My self many respected senior journalists associated with Bol reviewing situation threadbare will make judgment soon..truth must prevail — Kamran Khan (@AajKamranKhan) May 23, 2015
Read: Senior journalist Kamran Khan resigns from Geo
Earlier, a political analyst tweeted that the two ‘main pillars’ of Bol network would tender their resignations soon in an apparent reference to senior journalists Kamran Khan and Azhar Abbas.
Two main pillars of Bol Network will tender their resignation very soon, insist my sources. — Murtaza Solangi (@murtazasolangi) May 23, 2015
As per my tweet of 19/05 on this issue MY position is very clear & same. I just need 2 talk 2 my sr editors and staff before announcing it — Azhar Abbas (@AzharAbbas3) May 23, 2015
Further, earlier, Wajahat Khan also tweeted he was consulting his mentors and family whether to stay at the network or not.
Thinking about it. Hard. Consulting with my mentors & fam. It's a tough call. We were supposed to change the system, not corrupt it further. — Wajahat S. Khan (@WajSKhan) May 23, 2015
Reacting to the news, many took a jibe at the journalists for not doing homework before joining the network.
